### Reasoning Effort

For reasoning models (e.g. `gpt-6-astra`, `gpt-6-sol`, `gpt-6-luna`), `reasoningEffort` controls how much internal reasoning the model performs before responding. Lower values are faster and cheaper; higher values produce more thorough answers.

```ts
import {
  openai,
  type OpenAILanguageModelResponsesOptions,
} from '@ai-sdk/openai';
import { generateText } from 'ai';

const result = await generateText({
  model: openai('gpt-6-astra'),
  prompt: 'Invent a new holiday and describe its traditions.',
  providerOptions: {
    openai: {
      reasoningEffort: 'low', // 'low' | 'medium' | 'high' | 'xhigh' | 'max'
    } satisfies OpenAILanguageModelResponsesOptions,
  },
});

console.log('Text:', result.text);
console.log('Usage:', result.usage);
console.log(
  'Reasoning tokens:',
  result.finalStep.providerMetadata?.openai?.reasoningTokens,
);
```

| Value | Behavior |
| --- | --- |
| `'none'` | No reasoning (GPT-5.1 models only) |
| `'minimal'` | Bare-minimum reasoning |
| `'low'` | Fast, concise reasoning |
| `'medium'` | Balanced (default) |
| `'high'` | Thorough reasoning |
| `'xhigh'` | Extra-high reasoning on supported models |
| `'max'` | Maximum reasoning on supported models |

Supported effort levels vary by model. Using an effort with an unsupported
model will result in an error.

You can control the amount of reasoning effort expended by o3-mini through the `reasoningEffort` parameter.
This parameter can be set to `low`, `medium`, or `high` to adjust how much time and computation the model spends on internal reasoning before producing a response.

